---
scope: .
description: >
  Canonical home of the AIDE methodology and the MCP server that delivers it — single source of truth for every downstream host project, with brain configuration owned by the user via a single editable file rather than by code-level backend registries.
intent: >
  This repository is the canonical home of the AIDE methodology — Autonomous
  Intent-Driven Engineering, with a deliberate second reading as AI Domain
  Expert — and the MCP server that delivers it to agents in host projects.
  Intent is the primary driver: a `.aide` intent doc governs the code, and a
  research agent plays the role of the human domain expert an engineering
  team would otherwise need to hire. The repo has two jobs: author the
  canonical docs, and ship them through the MCP handshake and aide_init.
  Success is an agent in a brand-new host project receiving the full
  methodology via tooling alone, then working intent-first — describing what
  it wants in cascading layers of abstraction and letting the agent turn
  those layers into code. The brain layer — the external knowledge store
  the methodology routes agents through for cross-project knowledge — is
  configured per host project by a single user-editable file the host owns
  and edits directly, not by a registry of connector identities baked into
  the server's code. Full autonomy through intent-driven development, with
  AI domain experts filling the expertise gap and brain wiring under direct
  user control, is the north star every other decision in this repo must
  serve.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - >-
      The canonical AIDE methodology — spec, template, progressive disclosure,
      agent-readable code, automated QA — lives in this repo as editable doc
      files and is the single source of truth. Editing those files is the
      only way to change what downstream host projects receive.
    - >-
      Two delivery channels, each with one clear job: MCP tool descriptions
      teach AIDE at handshake time; aide_init installs a short pointer stub
      into the host's config file, lands the progressively-disclosed
      methodology doc hub onto the host's disk, scaffolds pipeline slash
      commands, wires the MCP server into the host project, and configures
      IDE preview. Together they cover the full lifecycle from handshake
      to steady-state work. The stub-plus-hub shape of the methodology
      delivery is load-bearing: every session in the host pays the stub
      cost on every read of the config file, but pays the full doctrine
      cost only when the task actually touches `.aide` files. Progressive
      disclosure applies at the delivery layer, not just inside the docs.
    - >-
      Every downstream artifact the server ships — tool descriptions,
      host-installed pointer stubs, host-installed methodology docs,
      slash command templates — is composed from the canonical doc
      files at build or init time, never duplicated as an inline string
      or hardcoded template.
    - >-
      A host project that runs aide_init once is AIDE-ready: the installing
      agent did not have to paraphrase, remember, or quote any AIDE rules to
      get there, because the tooling carried the full methodology into the
      project.
    - >-
      The repo itself exemplifies AIDE because the server is written using
      it: progressive disclosure in the folder tree, agent-readable names,
      `.aide` specs next to orchestrators, decisions-not-descriptions in
      every spec. Cascading intent at every level, so a contributor can
      write a new submodule spec by inheritance without re-deriving the
      rules.
    - >-
      Brain configuration in every host project lives in a single
      user-editable file at the host's `.aide/config/brain.aide` — the
      canonical and only source of truth for the host's brain layer.
      Its frontmatter declares exactly two things: a human-readable
      `name` for the wired brain (the user and the agent read it as a
      label; the package never branches on it) and a `mcpServerConfig`
      object naming the launcher command and args (consumed by sync,
      written into `.mcp.json`). Every other agent-facing concern —
      which MCP tool to call for reads, searches, writes, navigation;
      where to start; how to follow links — lives in the hand-written
      body sections, owned per consumer (the prose section, the playbook
      section, the research section — see the body-schema outcome
      below). The default scaffolded `brain.aide` makes no use of
      substitution: `mcpServerConfig.args` carries the path inline,
      byte-for-byte under user ownership. The parser still supports
      `${...}` interpolation of frontmatter fields inside
      `mcpServerConfig.args` for advanced users who want to DRY a value
      across positions, but that surface is not exercised by the default
      scaffold. The body sections are hand-written verbatim per backend
      — no templating, no rendering, no agent-facing substitution.
      Adding, replacing, or retargeting a brain is a host-side edit to
      one file followed by a sync, never a code change to this package.
    - >-
      The body of `brain.aide` is parsed as exactly three named sections
      — `prose`, `playbook`, and `research` — delimited by paired
      HTML-comment markers that the parser recognizes case-sensitively
      and lowercase-only. The recognized markers, in required order, are
      the `<!-- aide-prose-start -->` / `<!-- aide-prose-end -->` pair,
      the `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-playbook-end -->`
      pair, and the `<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `<!-- aide-research-end -->`
      pair — six markers total, three pairs, each section opened and
      closed by its own distinct tokens. The convention extends the
      existing `writeMethodology` HTML-comment marker precedent (same
      lowercase shape, distinct open/close per section). Marker layout
      violations are hard failures returning `malformed-body` naming the
      violating marker — missing pair, malformed or typo'd marker,
      unmatched close without prior open, open without close, wrong
      section order, nested markers all halt the pipeline. Bytes outside
      any marked section — between frontmatter and the first opener,
      between any closer and the next opener, after the last closer —
      are silently ignored by the parser; the parser's job is to
      extract the three named sections, not to police the rest of the
      file, so users may keep notes-to-self, scratch text, or comments
      in those regions without warning or error. Pre-pivot `brain.aide`
      files using the old heading-based body schema (`## Prose` /
      `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub` with no markers) return
      `malformed-body` naming the missing markers — there is no
      transitional read path; users hand-edit, consistent with the
      existing strict-failure migration invariant for body schema
      changes.
    - >-
      `brain.aide` is a documented AIDE file type sitting alongside
      `intent.aide`, `plan.aide`, `todo.aide`, and `research.aide` in
      the canonical methodology — with its own dedicated methodology doc
      under `.aide/docs/` describing its schema, lifecycle, and the
      explicit divergence that it is a config file, not an intent spec
      (no `scope`, no `outcomes`, its frontmatter is a typed config
      object and its body is hand-written agent-facing prose). The
      methodology doc ships from this repo to every host project the
      same way every other canonical doc does.
    - >-
      The MCP server never dispatches on connector identity. `aide_brain`
      reads the host's `brain.aide` and returns its prose body verbatim
      alongside a status — no server-side substitution at the prose
      layer, no rendering pass, no template engine. The response shape
      carries status and instructions only — no `backend` field, no
      connector label, no enum the server interprets. What brain is
      wired is information for the user and the agent reading the
      literal prose, not for the server's control flow. Different
      connectors ship structurally different prose; sharing prose across
      connectors via templating would force a uniformity their actual
      instructions do not have.
    - >-
      `aide_init` ships a ready-to-use brain out of the box: it scaffolds
      `.aide/config/brain.aide` pre-filled with a canonical Obsidian
      configuration that launches via `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`, so a host
      running the standard install has a working brain UX the moment
      init finishes — no extra step, no regression in default-install
      ergonomics. Users who want a different brain edit one file and
      run sync.
    - >-
      `.aide/config/` is the canonical home of user-owned configuration
      in every host project. Files inside are scaffolded once at
      `aide_init` if absent, then belong to the user forever — neither
      `aide_init` nor `aide_upgrade` overwrites, patches, or migrates
      anything under `.aide/config/` on subsequent runs. Files outside
      `.aide/config/` (the methodology doc hub at `.aide/docs/`,
      pipeline command templates, agent definitions, skills, every
      other artifact the package ships) are package-owned and
      re-installed on every init/upgrade. The boundary is the directory
      path itself — install/upgrade tooling reads the path as the
      ownership signal, with no per-file allowlist. `brain.aide` is the
      current inhabitant; the convention is forward-compatible with
      future user-owned config files.
    - >-
      `brain.aide` carries no `rootPath` field — the brain's location
      lives exactly once, inline in `mcpServerConfig.args`. The package
      never derives a logical root from args, never stats a path to
      validate it, never introspects args to infer connector identity.
      Path validation is the launcher's job at MCP server startup; the
      package does not second-guess it. Agents call MCP tools with
      vault-relative paths and trust the server's responses. Boot
      reporter brain status states are reduced to `ok`, `no-brain-aide`,
      `no-mcp-entry`, and `mcp-drift` — the prior `invalid-path` state
      goes away with `rootPath`.
    - >-
      `brain.aide`'s frontmatter contains exactly two top-level fields:
      `name` (string, the human-readable label for the wired brain) and
      `mcpServerConfig` (object with `command` and `args`, consumed by
      sync to write the `.mcp.json` brain entry). No `connector`, no
      `rootPath`, no `entryFile`, no `tools` — the schema is the
      minimum that still has real consumers. `name` is a label; the
      package never branches on it. Different brains may share names
      without affecting code behavior. The label exists for the human
      reading the file and for the agent narrating, in conversation
      with the user, which brain it is connected to.
      `mcpServerConfig.command` and `mcpServerConfig.args` mirror the
      shape `.mcp.json` expects under `mcpServers["brain"]` so the sync
      verb can write them through unchanged; the args list carries the
      launcher invocation and the path or identifier inline.
    - >-
      The brain's interface contract is the prose body, not a
      frontmatter declaration. There is no frontmatter field declaring
      expected MCP tool names, expected operations (read, search, write,
      list), or expected entry-point paths — the schema does not
      enumerate any of those. The agent reads the prose verbatim, calls
      whatever MCP tools the prose names, and surfaces failures
      (missing tool, wrong shape, unwired backend) to the user at the
      first call. Failure is fast, specific, and unmistakable: a user
      whose MCP session does not expose what their prose names finds
      out immediately, not after passing a parser check that lied.
      Placing the binding cost in one place — the prose body — instead
      of forcing the user to keep frontmatter declarations and prose
      in sync is the whole point. The schema's minimum justifies its
      maximum: every frontmatter field must name a real runtime
      consumer (sync writes `mcpServerConfig` into `.mcp.json`; the
      user and agent read `name` as a label). A frontmatter field with
      no code path that consumes its value is validation theater and
      is rejected.
    - >-
      `brain.aide` and the host's `.mcp.json` brain entry stay in sync
      through one explicit, user-driven action — `npx aidemd-mcp sync` —
      that reads `brain.aide` and writes the corresponding `.mcp.json`
      brain entry. Sync is idempotent. The MCP server never silently
      rewrites `.mcp.json`; mutation of the host's MCP wiring is always
      a deliberate user action with a visible command boundary.
    - >-
      Drift between `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` is a hard halt for the
      pipeline. The boot reporter detects drift and surfaces it as the
      `mcp-drift` brain status state, which the orchestrator treats the
      same way it treats a missing MCP entry — the pipeline does not
      proceed until the user runs sync. The brain status state set is
      `ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, and `mcp-drift` — covering
      the healthy case, the absence of `brain.aide`, the absence of the
      `.mcp.json` brain entry, and the drift case respectively.
    - >-
      Storage-agnostic vocabulary across every surface this package
      authors. The forbidden-token list — `vault`, `obsidian`, `note`
      (used as a synonym for "file"), `wikilink`, `hub note`, and `hub`
      — must not appear in any user-facing prose surface (MCP tool
      descriptions, the README, the canonical methodology docs under
      `.aide/docs/`, slash-command prompts under `.claude/commands/`,
      agent definitions under `.claude/agents/`, and every `.aide`
      spec authored in this repo) nor in code identifiers, comments,
      or string constants project-wide. The brain layer is described
      to agents and users in storage-agnostic terms — "brain", "brain
      root", "the brain's prose section", "the playbook section", "the
      research section" — never in the vocabulary of any one connector.
      Three exceptions are explicitly allowed because each names a
      legitimate non-branching anchor rather than a code or prose
      decision: the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper folder name
      (describes bundled template content, not a dispatch axis), the
      `.obsidian` directory heuristic comment in `brainRootExists` (an
      honestly-labeled implementation detail in a single helper), and
      the legacy `obsidian` MCP key referenced inside migration
      branches (the key being migrated away from). Anti-example
      occurrences inside undesired-outcome blocks of specs are also
      allowed — naming a forbidden token to forbid it is the
      mechanism, not a violation. Every other appearance of the
      forbidden tokens, in code or prose, is a regression.
    - >-
      The in-code backend registry — matchers, drivers, identity inference
      — is fully retired. There is no module in this package whose job is
      to enumerate connectors, infer connector identity from MCP entries,
      or dispatch behavior on a connector enum. Adding a new brain is a
      user-side edit, never a code-level extension to this package.
  undesired:
    - >-
      A rule, convention, or doctrine that lives anywhere other than the
      canonical doc files — a commit message, a README, a code comment, a
      per-project CLAUDE.md edit. If the change is not in the canonical
      docs, it is not shipped, and it will drift away from whatever the
      server actually delivers.
    - >-
      Dual sources of truth: the same doctrine appearing in both this repo
      and the original brain notes without one being marked authoritative
      and the other deleted. Migration from the brain is a one-way move on
      the same commit.
    - >-
      A delivery channel other than MCP tool descriptions and aide_init —
      a sixth tool that "also explains things," a server-side prompt, a
      standalone CLI that teaches. Every additional channel fragments the
      teaching surface and forces agents to guess where to look.
    - >-
      A canonical doc that describes behavior the server does not actually
      deliver. Aspirational docs are worse than missing docs, because
      missing docs do not lie.
    - >-
      A change that makes the cascading-intent model less faithful, less
      complete, or less teachable — a child spec restating its parent, a
      parent so narrow that its children have nothing to inherit, a helper
      with a `.aide` file, an orchestrator without one. These are
      regressions even when they add features.
    - >-
      Any code path in this package that branches on connector identity —
      a switch on `connector`, a registry lookup keyed by name, a matcher
      that maps an MCP entry to a "known backend" so dispatch can pick a
      driver. The server reads `brain.aide` and returns its prose body
      verbatim; it does not know nor care what brand of brain is wired.
    - >-
      A `brain.aide` whose schema borrows intent-spec frontmatter
      conventions — a `scope` field, an `intent` paragraph, an `outcomes`
      block, a `status` lifecycle field. `brain.aide` is a config file,
      not an intent spec; conflating the two erodes the distinction
      between intent contracts and runtime configuration and confuses
      every downstream agent that has to decide which rules apply.
    - >-
      The MCP server silently rewriting the host's `.mcp.json` to "fix"
      drift, "auto-sync" on boot, or repair a missing brain entry. Every
      mutation of the host's MCP wiring must be an explicit user action
      run through the sync verb; auto-repair shifts a load-bearing
      contract surface out from under the user.
    - >-
      `aide_init` or `aide_upgrade` overwriting, patching, migrating, or
      otherwise mutating any file under `.aide/config/` on a subsequent
      run. Files in that directory are user-owned the moment they are
      scaffolded; any code path that touches them after their first
      write — even to "fix" a stale template, land newer defaults, or
      tidy formatting — shifts a load-bearing user surface out from
      under the user. Same class of failure as the MCP server silently
      rewriting `.mcp.json`. New defaults reach existing hosts only
      through the sync verb or a deliberate user edit, never through
      the install or upgrade path.
    - >-
      Any code path that introspects `mcpServerConfig.args` to extract
      a vault path, infer connector identity, or stat a derived path.
      Path validation is the launcher's job at startup; the package
      does not second-guess it. Reintroducing a `rootPath` field, an
      `invalid-path` boot state, or any per-connector path validation
      is the same regression in different clothing — the brain
      refactor's no-connector-branching invariant must hold even
      against helpers that look like generic plumbing.
    - >-
      Any frontmatter field added to `brain.aide` whose only consumer
      is the parser's own validation — `tools.*` declaring expected
      MCP tool names the runtime never actually checks; `entryFile`
      naming a path no code reads; `connector` declaring a backend
      identifier the server is forbidden from branching on; `rootPath`
      shadowing a value already inline in args. This is validation
      theater: a field that exists to nag the user into filling it in,
      with no code path that consumes the value once filled. The
      schema must justify each field by naming a real runtime consumer
      (sync writes `mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name`
      as a label). Anything else belongs in the prose body where the
      agent actually reads it, or nowhere at all.
    - >-
      Connector-specific prose hardcoded into the package — Obsidian
      launcher snippets baked into `aide_brain`'s response, Notion-shaped
      examples compiled into the rendered output, vault-path conventions
      assumed in the boot reporter. The only place connector-specific
      content lives is inside `brain.aide` itself, where the user owns
      and edits it.
    - >-
      Server-side substitution applied to the prose body — placeholder
      rendering, template engines, variable interpolation, anything that
      mutates the prose between disk and `aide_brain`'s response. The
      prose body is hand-written per connector and ships verbatim;
      introducing a substitution pass conflates the install-time
      interpolation surface (`mcpServerConfig.args`) with the
      agent-facing surface and forces every prose author to think about
      which strings will be rewritten. Substitution lives only in
      `mcpServerConfig.args` (install-time), never in the prose body
      (agent-time).
    - >-
      Boot reporting that reduces the post-refactor brain layer to a
      single binary state — wired or not. Without explicit states for
      "no `brain.aide`" and "drift between `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json`,"
      the pipeline cannot tell the user what to do to recover, and the
      orchestrator cannot enforce the hard halt the sync contract
      depends on.
---

## Context

Before this repo existed, the AIDE methodology lived in a personal Obsidian
vault. That worked for one maintainer but could not ship: every new project,
every new contributor, every new agent framework had to rediscover the rules
from a vault they did not have access to, and any CLAUDE.md snippet that
tried to carry the rules forward went stale the moment the canonical vault
notes changed.

The MCP handshake is the only documentation surface a compliant MCP client
is required to present to the model verbatim, in every session, in every
project, without user action. aide_init is the only command needed to turn
a cold host project into one that knows AIDE. Together those two channels
give the methodology a delivery path that does not depend on the agent
remembering anything across sessions or the user editing any file by hand.

This repo's job is to author the methodology once, hold it as the single
source of truth, and ship it through those two channels. Nothing else.

The brain layer — the external knowledge store the methodology routes
agents through for durable, cross-project knowledge — used to be wired
into this package by code. The server enumerated connectors, inferred
which one a host had wired by sniffing the shape of an MCP entry, and
returned connector-specific prose composed in the package's own source.
That contradicted the portability commitment the methodology itself
makes: every host gets to run AIDE the same way, against whatever
knowledge store fits its situation, without this package having to be
extended every time a new store appears. The refactor moves brain
configuration out of the package's code and into a single user-editable
file the host owns. The package no longer has an opinion about which
brain a host runs against; it has an opinion about how the host
declares it.

## Strategy

**Canonical docs are editable files, not compiled output.** The doc set
lives in this repo as plain Markdown the maintainer can read, diff, and
edit. Every downstream artifact — tool descriptions, methodology blocks,
slash command templates — is composed from those files at build or init
time. A doctrine change is one edit in one file; propagation to every
downstream host project is automatic on their next init. If a maintainer
ever has to edit two places to ship one change, the source-of-truth
invariant has been broken and must be repaired, not worked around.

**Two delivery channels, no third.** MCP tool descriptions teach AIDE at
handshake time: what `.aide` files are, where they live, the naming rules,
the read-before-code workflow. aide_init installs the rest: a short
pointer stub into the host's config file, the full canonical methodology
doc set into a dedicated host-side doc hub the stub points at, the slash
commands that run the pipeline, the MCP wiring, and the IDE preview. The
two channels are cleanly partitioned — handshake teaches concepts, init
installs artifacts — and between them they cover the lifecycle an agent
in a host project actually walks. Any proposal to add a third channel
should be rejected and redirected into improving one of the existing two.

**Methodology delivery honors progressive disclosure at the delivery
layer.** The agent reads the host's config file on every session, so
that surface carries only a short stub that names the installed doc hub
and teaches the crawl trigger. The full canonical methodology lands as
files on the host's disk, read on demand by sessions that actually touch
`.aide` material. This mirrors what the user's global `study-playbook`
skill does against the brain — a shallow pointer surface naming a deep
content surface, with the agent crawling only when the task requires it.
Shipping the full methodology body inside the stub region violates the
pattern the methodology itself teaches, which is exactly the regression
this architecture exists to prevent.

**The repo eats its own dog food.** The MCP server is written using AIDE.
Progressive disclosure in the folder tree, folders named after their
default exports, JSDoc on every function, inline narration only on
orchestrators, `.aide` specs next to orchestrators and nowhere else,
decisions-not-descriptions in every spec. This is not stylistic — it is
the forcing function. A rule that is wrong or unworkable shows up as
pain in this repo before it ships to a host project. If a rule can't
survive being lived under here, it doesn't belong in the canonical docs.

**Migration from the brain is one-way.** Every doc that lands in this
repo is deleted or archived from the brain on the same commit. Drift
between two copies of a canonical doc is the worst kind of drift,
because it silently propagates to every host project on its next init
and no one notices which copy is authoritative.

**Brain configuration is user-owned, not code-owned.** The architectural
boundary is sharp: the server reads the host's `brain.aide`, parses its
body as exactly three named sections — prose, playbook, research, each
delimited by its own paired HTML-comment markers — and never branches
on connector identity. The marker pairs follow the same lowercase shape
the existing `writeMethodology` stub uses, extended with distinct open
and close tokens per section so a parser violation names the offending
marker by name rather than guessing where a single-token region was
supposed to start. The agent-facing contract is the prose section: when
`aide_brain` is called, the parser extracts the prose section and
returns it byte-identical. There is no module in this package whose job
is to enumerate brains, infer which one a host has wired, or dispatch
behavior on a connector enum — the in-code backend registry is retired
entirely. The package ships zero connector-specific code paths. What
replaces the registry is a single file in the host's own repo: a typed
config frontmatter the host edits, and three hand-written body sections
the host edits. Adding a new brain is never a code change to this
package; it is a host-side edit plus a sync. The default scaffolded
`brain.aide` makes no use of substitution — `mcpServerConfig.args`
carries the path inline, byte-for-byte under user ownership, and the
package never derives a logical root from args, never stats a path to
validate it, and never introspects args to infer connector identity.
The parser still supports `${...}` interpolation of frontmatter fields
inside `mcpServerConfig.args` for advanced users who want to DRY a
value across argument positions; that surface remains for them, but it
is not exercised by the default scaffold. Substitution lives only in
`mcpServerConfig.args` at install/sync time, applied by the `.mcp.json`
writer when sync runs — never in any of the three body sections at
agent time. All three body sections return verbatim, with no rendering
pass between disk and the agent. The decision to hand-write the prose
per connector rather than template it is load-bearing: different
connectors need structurally different agent instructions, not the
same instructions with values swapped. One brain's prose may instruct
the agent to "read the entry file at the brain root and follow the
inline references it names to deepen context," another's may say
"query the indexed database for tagged pages and follow backlinks via
the hosted API"; templating cannot bridge that shape difference without
forcing both into a uniformity neither actually has. The mutation
surface for `.mcp.json` is `npx aidemd-mcp sync` and only `npx aidemd-mcp
sync` — the MCP server NEVER silently rewrites the host's MCP wiring,
because every load-bearing contract surface needs a visible command
boundary the user controls. Drift between `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json`
is a hard halt: the boot reporter detects it, surfaces it as an
explicit brain status state alongside the absence-of-`brain.aide`
state and the preexisting healthy and broken-wiring states, and the
orchestrator refuses to advance until the user runs sync.

**Intent-driven development is the measurement.** Every change to this
repo — a new doc, a new tool, a revised description, a refactored helper
— is evaluated against one question: does this make "describe what you
want in cascading layers of abstraction, let the agent build the code"
more real or less real? A feature that adds surface but doesn't move that
needle is a regression in disguise. A change that tightens the cascading
intent model is load-bearing even when it looks like cleanup.

## Good examples

A maintainer edits `docs/aide-spec.md` to clarify how outcomes cascade
from parent to child specs. They commit. On the next release, every
host project that runs `aide_init` sees the updated text land inside
its own `.aide/aide-spec.md` — the host-side doc hub contains a
byte-faithful copy of the canonical doc, so the edit reaches every
host project on its next init with no duplication anywhere in between.
Every agent that connects to the MCP server also sees the updated tool
descriptions (in whichever tools the rule touched), because those
descriptions were composed from the same doc file at build time. One
edit in one file, full propagation, zero paraphrasing, and the stub
inside each host's config file keeps pointing at the same hub path it
always did.

An agent in a cold host project is told only "run aide_init." It does,
reads back a summary naming the pointer stub in the config file, the
installed host-side doc hub with every canonical methodology doc by
name, the pipeline slash commands, the MCP registration, and the
IDE configuration, and then starts the pipeline with `/aide:research`.
At no point in that sequence did a human tell the agent what AIDE is,
what a `.aide` file is, or how the phases fit together — every piece
came through the tooling.

An agent opens a host project to implement a new feature unrelated to
AIDE. Its first read is the host's config file, which contains a short
pointer stub saying AIDE exists, naming the doc-hub path, and telling
the agent to crawl the hub before writing or acting on any `.aide`
file. The agent notes this, skips the hub for now because the task
touches no specs, and proceeds with the feature work carrying only a
handful of lines of AIDE context. An hour later, the same agent picks
up a task that does touch a `.aide` file; it follows the stub's
pointer into the hub, reads the hub index, drills into `aide-spec.md`
and `aide-template.md`, and leaves the rest of the canonical docs its
task does not need unread. The delivery layer practiced the same
progressive disclosure the methodology teaches — exactly the way the
user's global `study-playbook` skill crawls the brain on demand
instead of hoisting it into every session.

A contributor adds a new tool to the MCP server. They read `/.aide`,
narrow to `src/.aide`, narrow to their new submodule spec, and know
exactly which invariants their addition must honor: one-to-one mapping
between methodology phases and tools, client parity, idempotency,
progressive disclosure in the code, cascading intent in the spec.
A small number of reads, full contract, no source files opened.

A host runs `aide_init` and the installer scaffolds
`.aide/config/brain.aide` prefilled with the canonical default
configuration. The frontmatter has exactly the two fields the schema
requires — a human-readable `name` label and an `mcpServerConfig`
block sync writes verbatim into `.mcp.json`:

```
---
name: obsidian
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "D:/brains/my-brain"
---
```

`name: obsidian` is a label the user reads in the file and the agent
narrates back in conversation; the package never branches on it. The
body is hand-written and laid out as three marker-bounded sections in
the required order — prose first, playbook second, research third —
with each section opened and closed by its own pair of HTML-comment
markers:

```
<!-- aide-prose-start -->
Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools.
Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their brain-root-relative
path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries across the
brain. The brain's entry file is `CLAUDE.md` at the brain root — read
that first; it lists the inline references your task may need.
When a reference in the entry file looks relevant to your task, read
the file it names (depth 1) and check the references inside that file
too. Stay in scope; don't follow references into unrelated topics.
<!-- aide-prose-end -->

<!-- aide-playbook-start -->
The coding playbook lives at `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`.
Start there for any coding-conventions or patterns question; the
playbook is structured for top-down crawling.
<!-- aide-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-research-start -->
Domain research is filed under `research/` by topic. The entry file
at `research/research.md` lists the active research areas; drill in
when a task touches a domain you don't already have context for.
<!-- aide-research-end -->
```

The marker layout is what the parser enforces: lowercase, case-
sensitive, distinct open and close per section, in the prose-then-
playbook-then-research order. The body text inside each pair is
storage-agnostic — it teaches the agent the navigation pattern in
terms of "brain", "brain root", "entry file", and "references"
rather than naming any one connector. `aide_brain` returns the prose
section's bytes verbatim; `provisionBrain` reads the playbook and
research sections to scaffold the corresponding entry-file artifacts
on cold install. No code in this package interpreted any of the words
inside the markers; they are the user's instructions to the agent,
owned by the user, edited by the user. Bytes outside any marker pair
— a note-to-self above the first opener, scratch text between two
sections — are silently ignored by the parser; the parser's job is
to extract the three named sections, not to police the rest of the
file.

The user makes a one-line edit to their `brain.aide` — they retarget
the brain by changing the brain-root path string inline in
`mcpServerConfig.args` (the second list entry, sitting next to
`"@bitbonsai/mcpvault"`) and rerun `npx aidemd-mcp sync`. The sync
verb reads the file's frontmatter, takes `mcpServerConfig`
byte-for-byte as the user wrote it, and writes the resulting object
into `.mcp.json` under the `brain` key. The transcript is short —
sync reports that it read the config, names the brain key it wrote,
and exits. The user runs `npx aidemd-mcp sync` again immediately to
test idempotency: sync reads the same file, computes the same target
object, sees `.mcp.json` already matches, and exits reporting no
change. The host's MCP wiring was mutated exactly once, on a command
the user typed, with the user looking at the terminal.

## Bad examples

A doctrine change that lands as a one-liner in a host project's CLAUDE.md
after a maintainer "noticed something while using AIDE in production."
The host project is correct for a week. The canonical docs in this repo
still reflect the old rule. The next host project that runs aide_init
receives the old rule because aide_init reads from the canonical docs,
and the two projects now diverge invisibly. The fix is to land the
change in this repo first and let it flow outward, not to patch the edge
and forget to come back.

A `writeMethodology/` helper that builds its output by concatenating
hardcoded string literals instead of reading `docs/aide-spec.md`. The
canonical doc and the shipped methodology drift apart on the first edit
to either one, and a maintainer updating the doc has no way to know
their change did not ship. The repo has two sources of truth and
neither is marked authoritative.

An `aide_init` that skips the doc-hub step and dumps the full
concatenated methodology body inside the host's config file, the way
it used to before this architecture was locked in. The agent pays the
full doctrine cost on every read of the config file, the doctrine
crowds out the host's own content, and the methodology's own
progressive-disclosure rule is violated by the very tool whose job
is to install it. The refactor passed its tests and broke the
invariant it was supposed to enforce.

A pointer stub that names a host-side doc-hub path at `.aide/` while
the installer writes the hub into `aide/` (or any other mismatch of
the same shape). The agent reads the stub, follows the pointer, and
lands on a missing directory. The two sides were coincident string
literals instead of a single shared path source, and the refactor
shipped with its most load-bearing invariant broken at compile time.

A proposal for `aide_explain`, a new tool that "walks an agent through
the methodology interactively." The teaching surface now gains a third
delivery channel — handshake, install action, and now explain — and an
agent calling explain is an agent the tool descriptions failed to teach.
The correct response is to fix the tool descriptions, not to add a
channel that papers over their weakness.

A canonical doc that introduces a new pipeline phase — say, "review" —
without aide_init scaffolding an `/aide:review` slash command to match.
Agents read the methodology, look for the command, can't find it, and
conclude the system is incomplete. The doc was aspirational; the tooling
was real; they disagreed; the agent trusted the tooling and lost trust
in the doc.

A child `.aide` that opens with "this repo is the canonical home of
AIDE…" — a verbatim restatement of this spec's intent paragraph. The
child has earned nothing by copying; every token spent restating is a
token the child could have used to say what only it can say. Cascading
intent failed because the child didn't cascade.

`aide_brain`'s implementation peeks at `mcpServers["brain"]` in the
host's `.mcp.json`, inspects the entry's `command` and `args` shape,
matches the args against a registered table of "known launchers,"
identifies a specific connector, and returns connector-specific prose
hardcoded in the package's source. The package now has to know about
every brain a user might ever wire — the registry the refactor
retired comes back through the side door. A user who wires an MCP
server whose launcher shape doesn't match any registered matcher gets
a "no brain wired" status from the boot reporter even though they
clearly wired one; the package doesn't recognize their brain so it
pretends it isn't there. The host's brain configuration is no longer
user-owned; it is a vote the package gets to override. The whole
point of the refactor was to remove this branching, and re-introducing
it under any name — registry, matcher table, identity inferrer,
"connector detection helper" — is the same regression in different
clothing.

The boot reporter runs at server startup, detects that `brain.aide`
and `.mcp.json` disagree about the brain's launcher args, and
"helpfully" auto-syncs by overwriting `.mcp.json`'s brain entry from
the `brain.aide` source of truth. The user wakes up the next morning and
finds their `.mcp.json` mutated by something that was not a command
they typed. Worse: the prior afternoon they had hand-edited
`.mcp.json` to point the brain MCP server at a temporary debug
brain location while reproducing a bug; that hand-edit is now silently gone,
because the auto-sync treated `brain.aide` as authoritative without
asking. The user no longer trusts that the file they just edited
will still say what they wrote when they come back to it. The
substitution surface they thought they controlled — `.mcp.json` is
the host's file, mutated only on commands the user runs — has been
shifted out from under them. Drift is a hard halt, not a self-repair
event; the only thing the boot reporter is allowed to do with drift
is name it and stop.

`aide_brain` runs a substitution pass over the prose section before
returning it — the same `${...}` interpolation of frontmatter values
that lives in the `mcpServerConfig.args` install-time surface,
applied to one of the agent-facing body sections at response time.
The same regression with the substitution pass applied to the
playbook or research section instead of prose is the same regression
in different clothing — none of the three body sections get
agent-time substitution. Two failure modes follow. First: a connector
whose prose legitimately documents a templating language uses literal
`${...}` characters in the body — say, the prose explains that the
user's knowledge store lets them define page templates with
`${variable}` placeholders. The substitution engine eats those
characters and either errors or silently strips them, mangling the
agent-facing instructions in a way that's invisible from this
package's tests. Second, and more load-bearing: with substitution
in place, every body-section author has to track which strings will
be rewritten before delivery, and the temptation to "just template
the prose, playbook, or research sections too" lands. Two
structurally different connectors get forced into a shared template
that fits neither — Connector A's prose grows placeholders for
concepts only Connector B's agents need, Connector B's prose grows
placeholders for concepts only Connector A's agents need, both
bodies bloat with conditionals that exist only to keep the shared
template from breaking either side, and the user editing their own
`brain.aide` now has to learn the package's template DSL before they
can write instructions for their own brain. Hand-written body
sections per connector with zero agent-time substitution — across
the prose, playbook, and research sections alike — is the only
shape that scales without forcing connectors to agree on a
vocabulary they don't share.

## References

- `.aide/docs/aide-spec.md` -- canonical AIDE spec format conventions: required body sections, frontmatter rules, and the rule that strategy decisions trace to outcomes; informed the section integration and decision-form writing of the new strategy paragraph.
- `.aide/docs/index.md` -- current canonical doc set inventory; informed the implicit commitment that a new `brain-aide.md` doc will join the doc hub when the methodology adds `brain.aide` as a sixth file type.
- `src/service/install/writeMethodology/.aide` and `src/service/install/writeMethodology/index.ts` -- the existing HTML-comment marker precedent that the body-schema outcome extends; informed the lowercase, marker-bounded shape of the prose / playbook / research region delimiters and the principle that bytes outside the marker pair are not the parser's concern.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` -- the parser spec that consumes the body-schema decision; informed the direction that marker layout violations halt the pipeline with a `malformed-body` reason naming the offending marker, and that the parser extracts named sections rather than policing surrounding bytes.
- `.aide/handoff.aide` -- post-collapse architectural snapshot covering the four cycles that landed before this resync; informed which design decisions were preserved (per-consumer ownership, no agent-time substitution, no introspection of `mcpServerConfig.args`, the four-state boot reporter union) and which were tightened in the marker-pair pivot.

(No external research references were drawn for the brain-layer additions — that design was specified in conversation between the user and the orchestrator. The existing body's strategy decisions about methodology delivery were established in prior spec rounds and stand on the canonical doc set itself.)
